                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gas line upgrade if needed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Same for both</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Same for both</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Same for both</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Running cost</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lowest</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Work through a typical indoor replacement and the pattern is plain. Say the non-condensing unit is $700 cheaper. Its stainless vent costs perhaps $900 more than a PVC run, and the condensing unit needs a $100 neutralizer and a drain connection. The non-condensing project ends up a little more expensive on day one and then costs more to run every year. That is the result we see far more often than not, and it is why our cost guide warns that a non-condensing heater can be cheaper on the shelf and dearer installed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gas supply is a cost both share. Both types fire at up to roughly 199,000 BTU an hour in whole-house sizes, which is far more than a typical tank heater, and many homes need a 3/4-inch line run to the heater. Some condensing units, including Navien's NPE-A2 series, are rated to run on an existing 1/2-inch line for a limited length, up to 24 feet in Navien's case, which can save a retrofit, but that depends on the rest of the gas system. Our <a href="/what-size-tankless-water-heater-do-i-need/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">tankless sizing guide</a> explains why the BTU rating, not the box GPM, drives this.
          </p>

          <p>
            Long-term cost tips the same way. The condensing unit saves perhaps $35 to $50 a year in gas at average prices, and it is ENERGY STAR eligible for rebates. The non-condensing unit has one fewer heat exchanger to fail and no neutralizer to maintain, which is a small advantage in parts but not one that changes the arithmetic for most homes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Which Climates and Homes Suit Each Type
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cold-climate homes with an indoor install, basement, utility room or closet, should start with condensing. Cold groundwater means the secondary exchanger condenses hard all year, the unit is already indoors so there is no freeze risk to the drain, and the vent has to be built anyway. Size for winter groundwater, which in the northern states can drop into the 40s, and the larger condensing units with 199,000 BTU burners are where you will end up.
          </p>

          <p>
            Warm-climate homes that can mount the heater outdoors should seriously price non-condensing. In much of the South and Southwest, outdoor installation is normal, groundwater is warm enough that a smaller unit covers the house, and an outdoor non-condensing model with built-in freeze protection needs no vent and no drain. The installed price can be the lowest of any gas tankless option. Condensing outdoor units exist too, such as Rinnai's RX series with its outdoor vent accessory, but in a place that can freeze even occasionally their condensate line needs protecting.
          </p>

          <p>
            Homes with an existing, correctly sized stainless vent from a previous non-condensing tankless are the other good case for non-condensing. Replacing like for like on the same vent, if the manual for the new unit approves it, avoids most of the condensing advantage. Conversely, if the old vent is a brand-specific system that the new heater does not list, you are pricing a new vent either way, and condensing returns to the front.
          </p>

          <p>
            Households planning to stay a long time should lean condensing, because the efficiency gap compounds and Rinnai and Navien back their condensing heat exchangers for 15 years in residential use; Rinnai's non-condensing V65iN carries a 10-year heat exchanger warranty, 12 with an isolation valve kit. How long either type really lasts depends far more on water hardness and descaling than on the design, which our guide to <a href="/how-long-do-tankless-water-heaters-last/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">how long tankless water heaters last</a> covers in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, if you are still deciding whether gas tankless is right at all, compare honestly against the alternatives. A high-efficiency tank may be the better fit for a short ownership horizon, and in a mild climate with good electrical service a heat pump unit can beat both on running cost. Our <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best gas water heaters</a> and <a href="/best-heat-pump-water-heater/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best heat pump water heaters</a> guides cover those options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Shelf Prices</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A non-condensing heater looks $500 to $800 cheaper in the store. Indoors, a stainless vent routinely costs more than that difference, and the condensing unit then saves gas every year. Get both options quoted as complete installations, including vent, condensate drain and gas line, before deciding which heater is the bargain.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the Old Chimney Can Be Reused</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tankless heaters push exhaust under fan pressure and cannot use the Type B vent or masonry chimney an old tank heater used. Plan for a new vent route, and ask whether removing the tank orphans a furnace in an oversized chimney that now needs a liner, which can add $1,000 or more.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the Condensate Line Through Cold Space</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A condensate line that crosses an unheated crawlspace or garage wall can freeze, back up and shut a condensing heater down when you need it most. Keep it short, pitched and inside heated space, or insulate and heat-trace it. Check a condensate pump whenever you service the heater.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Neutralizer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Condensate is acidic enough to attack cast iron drains, concrete and some septic systems over time, and many local codes require neutralizing it. A limestone neutralizer costs $50 to $150 and a few dollars a year in media. Leaving it out to save money risks a failed inspection and drain damage later.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating Venting Rules as Universal</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some non-condensing units, such as Navien's NPN-U series, are approved for PVC, and some condensing units list specific polypropylene systems. Vent materials, lengths and terminations are set by each model's installation manual. An installer who works from habit rather than the manual can void the warranty and create a carbon monoxide risk.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a condensing tankless water heater worth the extra money?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most indoor installations, yes. A condensing unit costs roughly $500 to $800 more as a box, but it vents through PVC instead of stainless steel, which often costs more than that difference, and it burns roughly 15 percent less gas. Outdoors in a warm climate, where no vent is needed, a non-condensing unit can be the better buy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between condensing and non-condensing tankless water heaters?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A condensing tankless heater has a second heat exchanger that cools its exhaust until water vapor condenses, recovering extra heat. That raises efficiency to about 0.93 to 0.98 UEF and allows plastic vent pipe, but it needs a condensate drain. A non-condensing heater has one exchanger, hot exhaust, efficiency near 0.81 and usually stainless steel venting indoors.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a non-condensing tankless water heater use PVC venting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Most non-condensing tankless heaters produce exhaust hot enough to require sealed Category III stainless steel or a manufacturer-specified vent system. There are exceptions: Navien's NPN-U non-condensing series is approved for PVC, CPVC, polypropylene and stainless. Always follow the venting table in the specific model's installation manual rather than a general rule.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do condensing tankless water heaters need a drain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. They produce a steady trickle of mildly acidic condensate whenever the burner fires, which must be piped to a floor drain, standpipe or sink tailpiece, or to a condensate pump. Many jurisdictions and manufacturers call for a limestone neutralizer before the drain. If the condensate line blocks or freezes, the heater shuts itself down.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much more efficient is a condensing tankless water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Condensing models are typically rated about 0.93 to 0.98 UEF against roughly 0.81 for non-condensing units, so they use something like 15 percent less gas for the same hot water. In dollars that is commonly $35 to $50 a year for an average household, more for large families or where gas is expensive.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are condensing tankless water heaters less reliable?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in any way that shows up in warranties. Rinnai and Navien both cover their residential condensing heat exchangers for 15 years, and Rinnai's non-condensing V65iN carries 10. Condensing units add a secondary exchanger, a condensate trap and often a neutralizer, so there is slightly more to maintain, but descaling and water quality matter far more to lifespan.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I choose condensing in a warm climate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Indoors, generally yes, because venting cost drives the decision more than climate. Outdoors in the South or Southwest, an outdoor non-condensing model needs no vent and no drain, often making it the cheapest gas tankless install. Condensing units still condense in warm climates, because tap water arrives far below the exhaust dew point year-round.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

