        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For most indoor installations, buy a condensing tankless water heater. It costs more as a box, vents through cheap plastic instead of stainless steel, qualifies for ENERGY STAR rebates and burns roughly 15 percent less gas every year. Budget for a condensate drain and a neutralizer, and keep that drain line out of the cold.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Choose non-condensing when the heater can live outdoors in a warm climate with no vent, when a compatible stainless vent is already in place, or when you will not own the house long enough to collect on efficiency. In every case, decide on complete installed quotes, not on the price tag in the store.
          </p>
